WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.940
In a resting neuron, potassium leaks out and sodium leaks in, all the time.

2
00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:10.460
So why does the voltage hold still?

3
00:00:11.483 --> 00:00:15.803
Here is a made-up neuron, resting at about minus −71 mV.

4
00:00:16.666 --> 00:00:21.006
Potassium current leaves it all the time, about 200 pA.

5
00:00:21.926 --> 00:00:24.126
Sodium current comes in, about the same amount.

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:27.220
Neither ion is at equilibrium.

7
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.940
Yet the voltmeter needle does not move.

8
00:00:30.933 --> 00:00:32.233
Start with one term.

9
00:00:33.093 --> 00:00:34.993
Each ion has an equilibrium potential.

10
00:00:36.066 --> 00:00:41.246
It is the voltage where the electrical force on the ion balances its concentration

11
00:00:41.246 --> 00:00:44.286
gradient. There, the ion has no net push.

12
00:00:45.299 --> 00:00:46.899
Now picture a voltage scale.

13
00:00:47.819 --> 00:00:53.339
Potassium's equilibrium potential sits near the left end, at minus −95 mV.

14
00:00:54.233 --> 00:00:58.053
Sodium's sits near the right end, at about plus +60 mV.

15
00:00:58.916 --> 00:01:03.576
A marker on the scale shows the membrane potential, the inside minus the outside.

16
00:01:04.616 --> 00:01:08.496
The gap between the marker and an ion's own mark is the driving force.

17
00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:11.556
Its sign gives the direction of the current.

18
00:01:12.416 --> 00:01:13.596
Its size gives the strength.

19
00:01:14.599 --> 00:01:17.239
Tie a rope from the marker to each ion's mark.

20
00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:19.639
The rope's pull is that ion's current.

21
00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:22.869
More open channels make a thicker rope.

22
00:01:23.529 --> 00:01:26.309
That is conductance, how open the ion's path is.

23
00:01:27.249 --> 00:01:30.669
And a rope pulls harder the farther the marker sits from its mark.

24
00:01:31.633 --> 00:01:34.573
So current equals conductance times driving force:

25
00:01:35.616 --> 00:01:39.956
By convention, outward current, positive charge leaving, counts as positive.

26
00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:43.309
Our cell rests above potassium's mark.

27
00:01:44.149 --> 00:01:46.709
Which way does potassium flow through its open channels?

28
00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:58.499
Outward. The driving force on potassium is positive, so positive charge leaves.

29
00:01:59.499 --> 00:02:00.859
Sodium is the opposite.

30
00:02:01.819 --> 00:02:05.719
The marker sits far below sodium's mark, so sodium is pushed in, hard.

31
00:02:06.783 --> 00:02:09.963
Potassium has much more conductance, but a short stretch.

32
00:02:11.163 --> 00:02:13.383
Sodium has less conductance, but a long stretch.

33
00:02:14.383 --> 00:02:19.083
The two currents come out equal and opposite, about 200 pA each way.

34
00:02:20.083 --> 00:02:21.563
That is what rest means.

35
00:02:22.643 --> 00:02:24.903
Zero net current, not zero current for each ion.

36
00:02:25.916 --> 00:02:27.156
Two puzzles remain.

37
00:02:28.156 --> 00:02:30.336
Why does the cell rest here, and not somewhere else?

38
00:02:31.299 --> 00:02:33.259
And what if we push a little current in?

39
00:02:34.039 --> 00:02:36.679
How big a change, how fast, and how far does it reach?

40
00:02:38.999 --> 00:02:40.419
One rule answers both.

41
00:02:41.339 --> 00:02:46.079
Each ion's open channels pull the voltage toward that ion's equilibrium potential.

42
00:02:47.182 --> 00:02:50.042
Rest means the currents cancel, not that they stop.
